{"title":"Haulmark","sub":"A trucking company wants to buy a freight app its rivals use","type":"M&A","industry":"Logistics","geo":"United States","diff":3,"fmt":"Interviewer-led","time":"25-30 min","skills":"M&A · channel conflict · partner vs buy","prompt":"Redline is a large US trucking company. Its trucks often drive back empty. It wants to buy Haulmark, an app that matches shippers' loads with trucking firms, for $150M, to fill its empty return trips. The CEO asks whether the deal makes sense.","clarify":[{"q":"Haulmark's numbers?","a":"Revenue of $30M a year from fees on loads matched. Freight apps like it sell for about 4× revenue."},{"q":"Benefit to Redline?","a":"Priority access to loads on its return routes: about $8M a year of extra profit, which Redline values at 8×."},{"q":"Who uses Haulmark?","a":"About 60% of Haulmark's fees come from trucking firms that compete with Redline."},{"q":"Their reaction?","a":"In interviews, about half of those rivals say they would move to another app if Redline owned Haulmark."}],"structure":{"root":"Should Redline buy Haulmark for $150M?","branches":[{"head":"Haulmark on its own","items":["Revenue × multiple"]},{"head":"Value to Redline","items":["Filling empty return trips"]},{"head":"What changes after the deal","items":["Do rival carriers stay?"]},{"head":"Alternatives","items":["Commercial partnership"]}]},"hypothesis":"Haulmark is valuable because it is neutral. If Redline owns it, Redline's rivals may leave, and the app could lose much of its value.","questions":[{"q":"What is Haulmark worth to Redline, assuming nothing else changes?","exhibit":null,"answer":"<p>Standalone: $30M × 4 = $120M. Benefit to Redline: $8M × 8 = $64M. Total <b>$184M</b>, above the $150M price.</p>"},{"q":"Now factor in rival carriers leaving.","exhibit":{"kind":"chart","svg":"<svg xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\" width=\"399.26pt\" height=\"176.64pt\" viewBox=\"0 0 399.26 176.64\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" version=\